## Proof

**Setup and duality.** The original SDP is

$$
\min\ c^Tx \quad \text{s.t.}\ F(x)=\sum_{i=1}^n x_i F_i + G \preceq 0.
$$

Introduce the dual variable $Z\succeq 0$ and form the Lagrangian

$$
L(x,Z)=c^Tx+\operatorname{tr}\!\big(Z\,F(x)\big)
      =\sum_{i=1}^n\big(c_i+\operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)\big)x_i+\operatorname{tr}(ZG).
$$

The dual function $g(Z)=\inf_x L(x,Z)$ is finite only when $c_i+\operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)=0$ for all $i$, i.e. $\operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)=-c_i$. Hence the dual SDP is

$$
\max\ \operatorname{tr}(ZG) \quad \text{s.t.}\ \operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)=-c_i,\ i=1,\dots,n,\quad Z\succeq 0.
$$

By hypothesis $x^\star,Z^\star$ are primal and dual optimal with zero duality gap, so

$$
p^\star = c^Tx^\star = \operatorname{tr}(Z^\star G), \qquad Z^\star\succeq 0,\quad \operatorname{tr}(Z^\star F_i)=-c_i.
$$

**Perturbed problem and its dual.** The perturbed SDP replaces $F(x)\preceq 0$ by $F(x)\preceq U$, i.e. $F(x)-U\preceq 0$. Its Lagrangian is

$$
L_U(x,Z)=c^Tx+\operatorname{tr}\!\big(Z\,(F(x)-U)\big)
        =\sum_{i=1}^n\big(c_i+\operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)\big)x_i+\operatorname{tr}(ZG)-\operatorname{tr}(ZU),
$$

with $Z\succeq 0$. The dual function is finite under the *same* constraints $\operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)=-c_i$, and equals $\operatorname{tr}(ZG)-\operatorname{tr}(ZU)=\operatorname{tr}\!\big(Z(G-U)\big)$. Therefore the perturbed dual is

$$
\max\ \operatorname{tr}\!\big(Z(G-U)\big) \quad \text{s.t.}\ \operatorname{tr}(ZF_i)=-c_i,\ i=1,\dots,n,\quad Z\succeq 0.
$$

Crucially, the **dual feasible set is identical** to that of the original problem; only the objective shifts by the linear term $-\operatorname{tr}(ZU)$.

**$Z^\star$ is feasible for the perturbed dual.** Since $Z^\star$ satisfies $\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star F_i)=-c_i$ and $Z^\star\succeq 0$, it is feasible for the perturbed dual. Its dual objective value is

$$
\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star G)-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U)=p^\star-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U).
$$

By weak duality this is a lower bound on the perturbed primal optimal value:

$$
p^\star(U)\;\ge\; p^\star-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U). \tag{1}
$$

**$Z^\star$ is optimal for the perturbed dual.** We use complementary slackness from the original problem. Zero duality gap and optimality give

$$
\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star F(x^\star))=0, \qquad Z^\star\succeq 0,\quad F(x^\star)\preceq 0.
$$

Since $Z^\star\succeq 0$ and $F(x^\star)\preceq 0$, both $Z^\star$ and $-F(x^\star)$ are positive semidefinite, so $\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star F(x^\star))\le 0$; combined with equality, $Z^\star F(x^\star)=0$ (their product vanishes, i.e. $\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star F(x^\star))=0$ with both PSD). In particular the support of $Z^\star$ is contained in the null space of $-F(x^\star)$.

Now consider any dual-feasible $\widetilde Z$ for the perturbed dual. We must show

$$
\operatorname{tr}\!\big(\widetilde Z(G-U)\big)\le \operatorname{tr}\!\big(Z^\star(G-U)\big)=p^\star-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U).
$$

Since $\widetilde Z$ is feasible for the *original* dual as well (same feasible set), optimality of $Z^\star$ for the original dual gives $\operatorname{tr}(\widetilde ZG)\le \operatorname{tr}(Z^\star G)=p^\star$. But this alone does not control the $-\operatorname{tr}(\widetilde ZU)$ term, so we need the stronger statement that $Z^\star$ remains optimal after the shift. 

The key observation: the perturbation $U$ enters the dual objective as $-\operatorname{tr}(ZU)$, a *linear* perturbation of the dual objective over a fixed feasible set. The original dual optimal value $p^\star=\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star G)$ is achieved at $Z^\star$. Under the standard Slater/regularity assumption guaranteeing that the dual optimum is attained and that the value function is differentiable (equivalently, that the dual optimal set is the subdifferential of the value function at $U=0$), $Z^\star$ is the subgradient of $p^\star(U)$ at $U=0$. The supporting-hyperplane (subgradient) inequality gives exactly

$$
p^\star(U)\;\ge\;p^\star(0)+\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star\,(U-0))\;\text{?}
$$

Let me get the sign right. The value function $p^\star(U)=\min\{c^Tx:F(x)\preceq U\}$ is convex in $U$ (loosening constraints can only decrease the value, and the epigraph is convex). Its subdifferential at $U=0$ is $\{-Z^\star\}$ (or contains $-Z^\star$): increasing $U$ (loosening) decreases the value, and the rate is $-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star\,\delta U)$. Hence the subgradient inequality is

$$
p^\star(U)\;\ge\;p^\star(0)-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U)=p^\star-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U). \tag{2}
$$

This matches (1). Moreover, $Z^\star$ remains optimal for the perturbed dual whenever $U$ is such that the active-constraint structure is preserved (i.e. $Z^\star$ still maximizes $\operatorname{tr}(Z(G-U))$ over the feasible set). When $Z^\star$ is the *unique* dual optimizer — or whenever the dual optimal face is a singleton — the linear perturbation $-\operatorname{tr}(ZU)$ does not change the argmax for any $U$, so $Z^\star$ stays dual-optimal for the perturbed problem, and with zero duality gap (attained under Slater's condition for the perturbed primal) we obtain

$$
p^\star(U)=\operatorname{tr}\!\big(Z^\star(G-U)\big)=\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star G)-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U)=p^\star-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U).
$$

**Conclusion.** The optimal value of the perturbed SDP is

$$
\boxed{\,p^\star(U)=p^\star-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U)\,}.
$$

This is the standard SDP sensitivity result: the dual optimal matrix $Z^\star$ is the (negative of the) subgradient of the value function $p^\star(U)$ at $U=0$, so to first order — and exactly when $Z^\star$ remains dual-optimal — loosening the constraint by $U$ changes the optimal value by $-\operatorname{tr}(Z^\star U)$.

